    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "The short answer is **no, you should not make anything of it, and on its own, it is not a sign of bias or impropriety.** \n\nWhile it is completely understandable to look for patterns or explanations when you felt well-qualified for a leadership role, drawing conclusions based solely on the gender makeup of the panel is likely neither accurate nor helpful to your career.\n\nHere is an objective breakdown of why this is the case, along with how municipal hiring at this level typically works and what your best next steps are.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Demographics Are Usually Structural, Not Strategic\nIn local government, interview panels for department director roles are rarely handpicked to skew a demographic outcome. Instead, they are usually determined by executive hierarchy and role relevance. A typical panel might consist of:\n* The Chief Administrative Officer (CAO) / City Manager\n* The Director of Human Resources\n* A peer director (e.g., Director of Public Works or Community Services)\n* An external advisor, regional planning commissioner, or council liaison\n\nIf those leadership positions in that municipality happen to be held by women, the panel will naturally be all women. The composition alone is standard administrative reality, not evidence of in-group favoritism.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Qualifications at the Director Level\nAt the Director level, hiring criteria shift significantly from technical execution to political, strategic, and public leadership:\n\n* **Technical vs. Soft Skills:** While a master’s degree and professional planning license demonstrate technical mastery, executive municipal leadership often hinges on political acumen, media relations, conflict resolution, and council relations.\n* **Public Consultation:** Controversy in municipal planning almost always centers on public opposition (e.g., zoning changes, high-density developments, master plans). A candidate with deep experience de-escalating community opposition and facilitating public hearings can be viewed as an exceptional asset for a municipality that has experienced community friction.\n* **Press Releases Are Abridged:** Public announcements summarize a few bullet points chosen by the communications team; they rarely capture the full scope of a candidate’s career, leadership history, or how they performed in their interviews.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay on Feedback\nA two-week wait for written feedback in municipal government is very common and not necessarily a sign of bad faith:\n* **Public Sector Bureaucracy:** Once an offer is accepted, HR is typically occupied with background checks, contract negotiations, council notification, and administrative onboarding.\n* **Legal Caution:** Many public sector HR departments run written feedback past legal counsel or require strict boilerplate responses to avoid opening the municipality to formal appeals or litigation.\n* **Panel Sign-off:** Compiling written notes from four different senior leaders takes time, especially given their day-to-day operational schedules.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Practical Advice and Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Avoid Bringing Up Demographics:** \n   Raising the gender composition of the panel—even informally—without concrete evidence of discriminatory conduct (e.g., inappropriate interview remarks or documented procedural violations) is almost certain to backfire. Municipal planning networks are tight-knit, and doing so could harm your professional reputation across neighboring municipalities.\n2. **Follow Up Professionally for Growth:** \n   Send a brief, gracious follow-up email framed around self-improvement rather than questioning the outcome:\n   > *\"I understand you are busy finalizing the recruitment process. When time permits, I would be grateful for any brief developmental feedback the panel might have on how I can strengthen my profile for future executive opportunities.\"*\n3. **Offer an Informal Call:** \n   Panels are often hesitant to commit detailed critiques to writing for legal reasons, but a panel member or the HR lead might be willing to spend 10 minutes on a candid phone call to share verbal feedback.\n4. **Recognize the Achievement:** \n   Making the final shortlist of three and completing two rounds of interviews for a Director-level post is a strong validation of your credentials and reputation. You were clearly seen as fully capable of doing the job; in executive searches, final selections often come down to interview chemistry, leadership style alignment with the CAO, or specific immediate municipal priorities.\n",
